.TH CTI-TRIAGE 1 "2026-11-19" "cti-agent" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-triage \- turn one threat-intel advisory into facts from our own tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cti\-triage
.BR \-\-show\-input " | " \-\-read " | " \-\-facts " | " \-\-judge " | " \-\-show\-judge\-input
.B \-\-message
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-subject
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-sender
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-subjects\-file
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-facts\-file
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-judgement\-file
.IR F ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR D ]
.PP
.B cti\-triage \-\-batch
.I FILE
.B \-\-out
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR D ]
.RB [ \-\-batch\-timeout
.IR D ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-facts \-\-message
.I advisory.txt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cti-triage
is advisory triage run by hand (phases 3 and 4 of the triage redesign). It reads
one advisory, a text file holding its body, and writes nothing.
.PP
A model, the
.IR reader ,
is started with no tools, no MCP servers and no session file, and is shown
the advisory alone. It never sees the organisation's data. It answers with
the vendors, products, versions and ATT&CK techniques the advisory concerns.
Code then removes every claim the advisory does not support: a product,
vendor or fixed version not in the advisory text, a quote not in it word
for word, a technique that is not an ATT&CK ID. Techniques the reader added
that the advisory does not name are marked as inferred.
.PP
.B \-\-facts
then looks the rest up in Falcon: which installed applications match each
product, with host counts and about how many run a version older than the
fixed one; how many hosts saw each indicator (the domains, IPs and hashes
code extracted, and the hosts of defanged links); and how many Falcon alerts
named each technique in the last 30 days. Every value is re-checked for its
exact shape before it goes into a query, and lookups are capped per
advisory: 5 products, 25 indicators, 10 techniques.
.PP
.B \-\-judge
then hands the fact sheet, and never the advisory, to a second no-tools
model, the
.IR judge ,
which says for each subject
.BR likely ,
.BR possible ,
.B unlikely
or
.BR unknown ,
why, and which facts it relied on, by their IDs. Code checks the answer
against those facts: an invented or another subject's fact is dropped;
.B likely
without a host that runs the product or saw an indicator becomes
.BR possible ;
a reason that says whether we are affected or safe, or claims a sighting or
an installation it cites no fact for, is replaced; a subject the judge
skipped is
.BR unknown .
Code then raises to at least
.B possible
any subject whose product is installed and not known to be fixed, and every
subject when one of our hosts saw one of the advisory's indicators;
.B unlikely
becomes
.B unknown
when that subject's inventory lookup failed. Sightings are printed first,
by code, even when the judge fails or refuses.
.PP
.B \-\-batch
is how the daily digest runs it (TRIAGE_ENABLED, see
.BR run\-digest (1)):
every threat-intel message ingest saved. The reader lists each one's
items with their CVEs; items naming a CVE found in the message are left to
the CVE report and listed, and the rest go through the lookups and the
judge. A message where the reader finds nothing and code has nothing to look
up is marked not threat intel, and the judge is not asked. Each message has its own
.BR \-\-timeout ,
into one
.B cti\-advisories/1
report for
.BR brief.py .
Authenticated feed senders go first, then oldest first; at most
.B TRIAGE_MAX_PER_RUN
(default 10, at most 50) are triaged, and none is started after
.BR \-\-batch\-timeout .
Every advisory read is in the report: triaged, with any stage that failed,
or not triaged and why. A failed reader still leaves the indicators and the
advisory's techniques to look up; a failed judge still leaves code's verdicts
and the sightings. Without Falcon or the reader's instructions nothing is
triaged, every advisory is listed as such, and the exit status is 1.
.PP
Every model call's tokens and cost are printed on stderr.
.SH OPTIONS
Exactly one of
.BR \-\-show\-input ,
.BR \-\-read ,
.BR \-\-facts ,
.B \-\-judge
or
.B \-\-show\-judge\-input
is required.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-input
Print what the reader would be shown. No model call, no lookups.
.TP
.B \-\-read
Run the reader and print its answer after the checks, with what was removed.
.TP
.B \-\-facts
Run the reader, then the Falcon lookups, and print the fact sheet.
.TP
.B \-\-judge
Run the reader, the lookups and the judge, and print the checked verdicts.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch " FILE"
With
.BR \-\-out :
triage every advisory in
.I FILE
.RB ( "cti\-agent \-\-advisories\-out" ).
Takes no other mode or
.BR \-\-message .
.TP
.BI \-\-out " FILE"
Where
.B \-\-batch
writes its report, 0600.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-timeout " D"
With
.BR \-\-batch :
start no advisory after this long. Default: 20m.
.TP
.B \-\-show\-judge\-input
With
.BR \-\-facts\-file :
print what the judge would be shown. No model call, no lookups.
.TP
.BI \-\-message " FILE"
The advisory's body, up to 256 KiB.
.TP
.BI \-\-subject " S"
.TQ
.BI \-\-sender " S"
Header lines for the reader, if known.
.TP
.BI \-\-subjects\-file " F"
With
.B \-\-facts
or
.BR \-\-judge :
use a saved reader answer instead of a model call.
.TP
.BI \-\-facts\-file " F"
With
.B \-\-judge
or
.BR \-\-show\-judge\-input :
use a saved fact sheet
.RB ( \-\-facts " " \-\-json )
instead of the reader and the lookups.
.TP
.BI \-\-judgement\-file " F"
With
.BR \-\-judge :
check a saved judge answer instead of making the call.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print JSON
.RB ( cti\-subjects/1 ,
.B cti\-facts/1
or
.BR cti\-verdicts/1 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " D"
Give up after this long. Default: 5m.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRIAGE_MODEL
The reader's model: an alias or a full id. Required for a model call.
.TP
.B CLAUDE_BIN
The claude CLI, if it is not on the path.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_READER_INSTRUCTIONS
The reader's instructions. Default:
.IR $FLEET_CODE/agents/triage-reader/CLAUDE.md .
.TP
.B TRIAGE_BLIND_SPOTS
What Falcon cannot see, repeated on every fact sheet.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_JUDGE_MODEL
The judge's model. Default:
.BR TRIAGE_MODEL .
.TP
.B TRIAGE_MAX_PER_RUN
With
.BR \-\-batch :
how many advisories are triaged. Default 10, at most 50.
.TP
.B TRIAGE_JUDGE_INSTRUCTIONS
The judge's instructions. Default:
.IR $FLEET_CODE/agents/triage-judge/CLAUDE.md .
.TP
.BR FALCON_BASE_URL ", " FALCON_CLIENT_ID ", " FALCON_CLIENT_SECRET
As for
.BR cti\-falcon (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done. A lookup that failed is shown on the sheet, not in the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
The advisory, the instructions or the reader's answer could not be read, the
model call failed, Falcon could not be reached, or the run timed out. With
.BR \-\-judge ,
a judge that failed, refused or gave an unreadable answer is also 1, after
the verdicts (code's alone) and the sightings are printed.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
What the reader would see:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-show\-input \-\-message adv.txt
.RE
.PP
The fact sheet:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-facts \-\-message adv.txt \-\-subject "Weekly threat roundup"
.RE
.PP
Replay a saved answer without paying for another call:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-read \-\-message adv.txt \-\-json > saved.json
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-facts \-\-message adv.txt \-\-subjects\-file saved.json
.RE
.PP
Judge a saved fact sheet, and see what the judge is shown:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-facts \-\-json \-\-message adv.txt > sheet.json
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-show\-judge\-input \-\-message adv.txt \-\-facts\-file sheet.json
.br
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-triage \-\-judge \-\-message adv.txt \-\-facts\-file sheet.json
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cti\-falcon (1),
RUNBOOK.md section 11k, SECURITY.md.
